# Sunnah Clarity — Project Context

## What this is

A modern, statically-generated website that explains traditional Sunni Islamic concepts to English-speaking laypeople — specifically topics frequently misunderstood or attacked by Wahhabi/Salafi-leaning voices. Covers bid'a, mawlid, dhikr, tasawwuf, madhabs, chains of narration, and the scholarly tradition. All content is original human-written text citing primary Islamic sources, with SeekersGuidance.org linked as "go deeper" references. Non-commercial, purely informative.

## Tech stack

- **Framework:** Next.js 15 (App Router) with full static export (`output: 'export'`)
- **Hosting:** Vercel (free tier)
- **Content:** MDX files in Git repository
- **CMS:** Keystatic (deferred to v2 — direct MDX editing for v1)
- **Search:** Pagefind (static client-side search)
- **Styling:** Tailwind CSS 4 + `@tailwindcss/typography`
- **Components:** shadcn/ui (selective: accordion, tabs, dialog, tooltip, sheet)
- **Animation:** Framer Motion (selective, LazyMotion ~5KB)
- **Analytics:** Plausible or Umami (privacy-respecting)
- **Fonts:** Newsreader (body), Playfair Display (headings), Amiri (Arabic), Inter (UI)

## Key files

- `docs/STATUS.md` — current project state (check FIRST every session)
- `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md` — system design and data flow
- `docs/DECISIONS.md` — why we chose what we chose
- `docs/REQUIREMENTS.md` — feature list and acceptance criteria
- `.cursor/rules/` — agent behavior rules
- `.cursor/prompts/` — review agent prompts (Reviewer, Security, QA) used by Cloud Agents

## Architecture summary

Content flows: Human writes MDX → Git commit → GitHub PR review → Vercel build → Next.js SSG reads MDX → static HTML + Pagefind index → Vercel CDN serves globally. No database, no API layer, no runtime content generation. Religious content integrity is enforced architecturally — every word baked into static HTML was written and reviewed by humans.

## Content integrity rule

No AI generates, paraphrases, or modifies theological text. AI may be used for code, design, and non-content tasks only. All religious content cites primary Islamic sources (Quran, hadith, classical scholars) and links to SeekersGuidance.org for depth.
